C = [
("h1", "16. What Paul Leaves Unsaid"),
("p", "Claim: Paul’s letters leave unsaid much that later churches have settled, and the volume does not supply it. A list of what the passages do not say is part of the position, since an ordering argued from Paul has to say where Paul stops."),
("sub", "16.1 What the passages do not say"),
("p", "The passages do not say how many elders a church is to have. They speak of “elders in every city” (Titus 1:5), of “the bishops and deacons” at Philippi (Philippians 1:1) and of “the elders that rule well” (1 Timothy 5:17), all in the plural, but they give no number. They do not say how the elders are chosen, except that Titus is to ordain them and that Timothy is to lay hands on no man suddenly. They do not give a rite of ordination, beyond the laying on of hands of the presbytery that Paul mentions as having accompanied Timothy’s gift (1 Timothy 4:14). Thomas Constable’s statement of the matter is apt: “Paul prescribed an organizational structure but left it flexible. He did not dictate the details but left these open for the local leaders to determine.”"),
("p", "They do not say who presides at the gathering or who chooses those who speak. They do not give an order of service. They do not say how long the meeting is, how often it is held or where. They do not say how a church is to decide a question that comes before it, and they do not mention a vote. They do not say how churches are related to one another, and they do not give any authority over a number of churches to any one man after the apostles’ delegates. They do not say what office, if any, a woman may hold. They say that Phebe is “a servant of the church which is at Cenchrea” (Romans 16:1), and they say that the deacons’ “wives” or women are to be grave (1 Timothy 3:11), and they leave the rest."),
("sub", "16.2 What the passages do not say about the gifts"),
("p", "They do not say when the gifts of 1 Corinthians 12 cease, and they do not say that they have. Paul says that “whether there be tongues, they shall cease” and that this will be “when that which is perfect is come” (1 Corinthians 13:8, 10), and he does not name the time. They do not say how the regulated gifts of chapter 14 are to be regulated in a church in which there is no speaking in tongues, or no prophesying. The volume has argued that the order of chapter 14 rests on edifying and on the character of God. It does not say that a church which has none of the gifts has broken the order."),
("sub", "16.3 What the passages do not say about the covering and the Supper"),
("p", "They do not say whether the women of the churches at all times are to cover their heads. Paul gives five grounds in 1 Corinthians 11 and no further instruction. They do not say, in the places this volume reads, how the Supper is to be conducted, since that is the subject of the Digital Study on the Lord’s Supper (Study 72) and the volume leaves it there. They do not say how the prayers and tongues are to be understood as prayer, which is the subject of the Digital Study on praying in the Spirit (Study 81)."),
("sub", "16.4 Why the volume says so"),
("p", "The point of the list is that an argument from Paul has to stop where Paul stops. A church may have a pastor, a board, a council or a vote, and each may be a sound arrangement, and none of them is Paul’s own sentence. The volume’s claim is that the grounds Paul gives are for the instructions he gives, and that where he gives no ground and no instruction, the volume does not make one. The reader may judge for himself what a church does where Paul is silent."),
("ctbl", "What Paul states and what he leaves unsaid.", [
    ("Matter", "What the passages state", "What they leave unsaid"),
    ("Elders", "They are ordained in every city; the qualifications", "How many; how chosen; a rite"),
    ("Deacons", "Qualifications; proved first", "Their duties"),
    ("The meeting", "Edifying; limits on number and turn; peace", "Who presides; the form; the length"),
    ("Women", "Pray and prophesy covered; silent in the judging; no teaching or usurping authority", "What office a woman may hold; the covering as a rule"),
    ("Discipline", "The aims; the church acts; Titus’s rule of two admonitions", "A fixed procedure; the church’s vote"),
    ("Gifts", "Set by God; the Giver’s purpose", "When any gift ceases"),
  ], [0.18, 0.42, 0.40]),
("sub", "16.5 What the volume takes from the chapter"),
("bul", [
    "Paul leaves unsaid the number and choosing of elders, the form of the meeting, and the relation of churches.",
    "The passages say nothing of when the gifts cease, and the volume names no date.",
    "The covering is not given as a rule in the passage; five grounds are stated.",
    "The volume does not supply what Paul does not say.",
  ]),
]
